WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.755
When building a database,

2
00:00:01.755 --> 00:00:03.390
there's often a lot
of different tables

3
00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:05.145
that you'd need to
consider including.

4
00:00:05.145 --> 00:00:06.690
But how do you determine what to

5
00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:08.700
include and what to exclude?

6
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:10.530
The answer is to identify

7
00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:13.575
the entities you're interested
in maintaining data on.

8
00:00:13.575 --> 00:00:16.140
In this video, you'll
learn how to explain

9
00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:18.585
the meaning of entities
in a relational database,

10
00:00:18.585 --> 00:00:21.030
differentiate between
attribute types,

11
00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:24.135
and be able to identify
entities and their attributes.

12
00:00:24.135 --> 00:00:27.225
Let's begin by exploring
what an entity is.

13
00:00:27.225 --> 00:00:29.700
An entity can be described
as an object that has

14
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.040
properties which define
its characteristics.

15
00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:34.050
An entity can be anything

16
00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:36.405
that represents a single
object in a database,

17
00:00:36.405 --> 00:00:38.650
such as a place or a person.

18
00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:40.905
In a relational database system,

19
00:00:40.905 --> 00:00:42.320
each interesting object in

20
00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:44.500
a project could be
considered an entity.

21
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:47.535
For example, a customer
or individual.

22
00:00:47.535 --> 00:00:50.360
An entity in a table is
comprised of rows and

23
00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:51.770
columns created in database

24
00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:54.025
management systems
such as MySQL.

25
00:00:54.025 --> 00:00:55.730
Let's explore this concept in

26
00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:57.800
more depth using the
example of a table

27
00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:59.270
that holds delivery records for

28
00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:01.820
the database of an
e-commerce store.

29
00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:04.490
The table name represents
the entity name,

30
00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:06.350
deliveries, and each column

31
00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:08.990
represents the
entity-related attributes.

32
00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:12.200
The system holds customer or
entity relevant attributes

33
00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:13.400
such as ID,

34
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.980
name, and delivery
status details.

35
00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:18.110
These attributes
hold relevant data

36
00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:19.895
about the table entity.

37
00:01:19.895 --> 00:01:22.460
Each instance of the
customer entity in

38
00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:24.020
this e-commerce system contains

39
00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:26.255
a record of data
about each customer.

40
00:01:26.255 --> 00:01:28.070
But there are also
different types of

41
00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:30.230
attributes in a relational
database system.

42
00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:32.060
These include simple attributes,

43
00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:35.705
composite attributes, and
single-valued attributes.

44
00:01:35.705 --> 00:01:38.210
There are also
multi-valued attributes,

45
00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:41.210
derived attributes,
and key attributes.

46
00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:44.000
Let's explore these attributes
in more detail using

47
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.590
the example of a student table

48
00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:47.710
in a relational database system.

49
00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:49.700
A simple attribute
is an attribute

50
00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:51.545
that cannot be
classified further.

51
00:01:51.545 --> 00:01:53.645
In the example of
the student records,

52
00:01:53.645 --> 00:01:56.050
the grade values cannot
be classified further.

53
00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:57.830
A composite attribute is

54
00:01:57.830 --> 00:02:00.500
an attribute that can be split
into different components.

55
00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:03.260
For example, the name value
of each student could be

56
00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:06.535
split into sub-attributes
such as first and last name.

57
00:02:06.535 --> 00:02:09.600
A single value attribute
can only store one value.

58
00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:11.390
In the student table example,

59
00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:13.100
the date of birth
column can only

60
00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:15.215
contain one value per student.

61
00:02:15.215 --> 00:02:17.090
These values can be defined

62
00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:19.180
as a single-valued attributes.

63
00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:21.470
With a multi-valued attribute.

64
00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:23.990
The attribute can store
multiple values in the field.

65
00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:27.170
For example, the student
email column could

66
00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:29.255
hold more than one
email per student;

67
00:02:29.255 --> 00:02:31.085
a college email address,

68
00:02:31.085 --> 00:02:33.145
and a personal email address.

69
00:02:33.145 --> 00:02:34.700
However, this practice should be

70
00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:36.470
avoided in a
relational database.

71
00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:38.300
A derived attribute is where

72
00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:41.090
the value of one attribute
is derived from another.

73
00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:43.280
In the student table, the age of

74
00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:44.480
each student can be derived

75
00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:46.450
from the respective
date of births.

76
00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:48.885
Finally, there's
the key attribute.

77
00:02:48.885 --> 00:02:51.305
This is a field that
holds a unique value

78
00:02:51.305 --> 00:02:54.095
used to identify
unique entity record.

79
00:02:54.095 --> 00:02:55.700
A good example, are the values

80
00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:57.965
contained in the
student ID column.

81
00:02:57.965 --> 00:03:00.560
Each ID is a unique
value which can be used

82
00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:03.125
to obtain data about
a specific student.

83
00:03:03.125 --> 00:03:05.240
Remember that there's
no point in considering

84
00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:06.440
entities or attributes that

85
00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:08.170
will not be used
in your project.

86
00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:10.115
You only need to capture data

87
00:03:10.115 --> 00:03:11.780
in your database
system that helps

88
00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:13.220
the users of your system

89
00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:15.800
complete certain
tasks and activities.

90
00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:17.930
You should now
understand the concept,

91
00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:20.510
entities in a relational
database and be able to

92
00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:24.640
differentiate between
attribute types. Good luck.